"""
BYOA Knowledge Retrieve Agent -- standalone GX10 service.

Reads from the same ChromaDB store that the ingestion service
(chromadb_ingest_service.py, deployed as byoa-rag-ingest3) writes to --
read-only from this agent's perspective, no ingestion logic here.

Unlike the ingestion service, this IS wrapped as a normal byoa_adk Agent
(capability: "knowledge-retrieve"), since retrieval is pure JSON
query-in/results-out and fits the existing Agent/TaskPayload pattern
cleanly -- no file upload involved, so there's no reason to bypass the ADK
the way ingestion had to.

Embeddings: vLLM only (both this agent's query-side embedding and the
ingestion service's document-side embedding) -- MUST stay the same
backend/model as chromadb_ingest_service.py, or vectors won't be
comparable in the same collection. Ollama is being phased out
platform-wide; no Ollama calls remain in this agent.

Session scoping: an optional `session_id` input restricts the ChromaDB
query to only chunks tagged with that session_id at ingest time (see
chromadb_ingest_service.py's /ingest, which accepts a session_id form
field). Used by the platform controller for goal-attached file uploads,
so a question about an uploaded file doesn't get diluted by unrelated
content already sitting in the shared knowledge base. Omitting session_id
searches the entire collection as before.

fetch_all mode: an optional `fetch_all` boolean input (requires
session_id) bypasses similarity search/ranking entirely and directly
fetches every chunk tagged with that session_id via ChromaDB's metadata
filter, sorted back into original document order (chunk_index) and
joined into a single `full_text` output field. This exists because
similarity search is the wrong tool for "summarize the whole document"
style goals against a large upload: a query like "summarize the document
X.pdf" has almost no semantic overlap with the document's actual content,
so it scores low and legitimately-present content gets reported as
fallback_recommended even though it's genuinely there. fetch_all sidesteps
that mismatch -- there's no ranking or threshold to fail. The single
full_text field also sidesteps a structural limitation of the platform
controller's $task_N.path reference syntax: it only supports static,
literal indices (results[0], results[1], ...) baked into the plan before
execution, so a downstream task can't dynamically reference "all N
chunks" when N (the chunk count) isn't known until this task actually
runs -- $task_N.full_text is one static reference regardless of how many
chunks the document has, the same pattern already used for small,
inlined uploads ($task_9001.text, see platform_controller.py).

Fallback design: this agent does NOT call the websearch agent itself, and
doesn't decide on its own to skip the knowledge base. It always searches
and always returns its own confidence signal --
`fallback_recommended` + `fallback_reason` -- so the platform controller's
Goal Expander can decide whether to *also* dispatch a websearch task, and
can explain *why* it did so, rather than silently switching sources.

Same .env-configurable / systemd / cloudflared conventions as every other
BYOA service on this box.

PERFORMANCE NOTE: the ChromaDB HttpClient is now created once at
import time and cached, instead of being reconstructed on every single
/run call. Measured locally: ~4x faster for the client-construction step
alone. Be aware this is a modest ABSOLUTE saving (a few ms), not the
explanation for the large
request-to-request latency swings seen in production -- those are almost
certainly vLLM embedding-inference time varying with GPU contention
across the shared v1/v2/v3 stacks, which this change does nothing to fix.
The collection object is still re-fetched from the cached client on every
call (cheap -- an in-memory lookup against an already-open client, not a
fresh file open), which sidesteps needing to assert this chromadb version's
collection objects are safe to share across waitress's worker threads.
If a newly-ingested document ever doesn't show up in retrieval results
without restarting this service, that would indicate the cached client
isn't picking up the ingestion service's writes promptly -- worth an
explicit test (ingest something new while this stays running, then query
for it) before trusting this long-term, since two separate processes are
writing to and reading from the same on-disk store.
"""
import os

import requests as _req
from dotenv import load_dotenv
from waitress import serve

from byoa_adk import Agent, DAG, Step
from byoa_adk.schema import TaskPayload, TaskResult, TaskStatus
from byoa_adk.server import build_flask_app

load_dotenv()

PORT = int(os.environ.get("PORT", "7406"))
BYOA_SECRET = os.environ.get("BYOA_SECRET", "byoa-dev-secret-change-in-prod")
os.environ["BYOA_SECRET"] = BYOA_SECRET
AGENT_ENDPOINT = os.environ.get("AGENT_ENDPOINT", "https://retrieve3.zackloh.dev")

CHROMA_DB_PATH = os.environ.get("CHROMA_DB_PATH", "/opt/byoa-rag-ingest3/chroma_data")
# The ChromaDB store is now owned by exactly one process: the standalone
# `chroma run` server (byoa-chromadb6.service). This service must NOT open
# its own direct PersistentClient onto CHROMA_DB_PATH's sqlite file --
# chromadb_ingest_service.py needs access to the same data, and two
# processes each independently holding a PersistentClient on the same
# on-disk store was the dual-writer bug that made ingested documents
# invisible to retrieval.
CHROMADB_SERVER_HOST = os.environ.get("CHROMADB_SERVER_HOST", "localhost")
CHROMADB_SERVER_PORT = int(os.environ.get("CHROMADB_SERVER_PORT", "4420"))
CHROMA_COLLECTION = os.environ.get("CHROMA_COLLECTION", "company_docs")

VLLM_BASE_URL = os.environ.get("VLLM_BASE_URL", "http://localhost:11501")
VLLM_EMBED_MODEL = os.environ.get("VLLM_EMBED_MODEL", "qwen3-embedding-8b")

DEFAULT_TOP_K = int(os.environ.get("DEFAULT_TOP_K", "5"))
# Cap on how much text a fetch_all=true request (see run()) will return in
# full_text -- protects downstream summarize-agent calls (and the LLM
# context window) from an unbounded blob for a very large document. If
# exceeded, full_text is truncated with a note appended rather than silently
# cut off.
MAX_FETCH_ALL_CHARS = int(os.environ.get("MAX_FETCH_ALL_CHARS", "40000"))
# Started at 0.55 as an untested guess. First real test against actual
# ingested data (a correctly-retrieved chunk containing the exact answer
# still only scored 0.44, partly due to a chunking issue since fixed, partly
# because terse/list-like factual content just scores lower than clean
# prose against a query). Lowered to reflect that -- treat this as a
# starting point to keep tuning as more real queries come in, not a
# permanent number.
DEFAULT_MIN_SIMILARITY = float(os.environ.get("DEFAULT_MIN_SIMILARITY", "0.40"))

# qwen3-embedding is an asymmetric retrieval model: per Alibaba's own model
# card (huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B), omitting an instruction
# prefix on the QUERY side measurably hurts retrieval accuracy -- documents
# intentionally get no prefix ("No need to add instruction for retrieval
# documents"), which is why the ingestion service's embed_texts() is
# correctly left as plain text and only this side changes.
QUERY_INSTRUCT_TASK = os.environ.get(
    "QUERY_INSTRUCT_TASK",
    "Given a search query, retrieve relevant passages from the company "
    "knowledge base that answer the query",
)

# Reused across calls instead of a fresh connection per request -- avoids
# repeated TCP setup overhead to the same local vLLM endpoint. Safe: no
# per-request state is stored on the session itself.
_http_session = _req.Session()


def embed_query(text: str) -> list:
    instructed_query = f"Instruct: {QUERY_INSTRUCT_TASK}\nQuery:{text}"
    r = _http_session.post(
        f"{VLLM_BASE_URL}/v1/embeddings",
        json={"model": VLLM_EMBED_MODEL, "input": instructed_query},
        timeout=30,
    )
    r.raise_for_status()
    return r.json()["data"][0]["embedding"]


# Created once at import time and reused -- see PERFORMANCE NOTE above.
_chroma_client = None


def _get_chroma_client():
    global _chroma_client
    if _chroma_client is None:
        import chromadb
        _chroma_client = chromadb.HttpClient(host=CHROMADB_SERVER_HOST, port=CHROMADB_SERVER_PORT)
    return _chroma_client


def get_collection():
    client = _get_chroma_client()
    # metadata here only matters if this process somehow gets to the
    # collection before the ingestion service ever creates it -- normally
    # ingestion is what actually establishes the collection (and its
    # cosine space). Included for symmetry/safety, not because this agent
    # expects to be first. Cheap: an in-memory lookup against the
    # already-open client, not a fresh file open.
    return client.get_or_create_collection(CHROMA_COLLECTION, metadata={"hnsw:space": "cosine"})


class KnowledgeRetrieveAgent(Agent):
    """
    Embeds a query, searches the company-docs ChromaDB collection, and
    returns the top matching chunks with similarity scores. Also reports
    whether the platform should fall back to web search -- and why -- when
    nothing found is a confident match.
    """
    name = "byoa-knowledge-retrieve-agent"
    version = "1.0.0"
    description = ("Searches whatever documents have been uploaded into this "
                   "platform's internal knowledge base and returns the most "
                   "relevant passages for a query -- covering any topic "
                   "those documents contain, not just company/business "
                   "content. Useful for any factual or informational "
                   "question where the answer might already be sitting in "
                   "an ingested document, before falling back to a live web "
                   "search. Accepts an optional session_id to scope the "
                   "search to only documents uploaded under that session "
                   "(e.g. files attached to the current goal), rather than "
                   "the entire knowledge base. Also accepts an optional "
                   "fetch_all flag (requires session_id) that bypasses "
                   "similarity search entirely and returns the ENTIRE "
                   "session's content, in original document order, as a "
                   "single full_text field -- use this instead of a normal "
                   "similarity query for goals asking to summarize/describe "
                   "a WHOLE uploaded document, since a query like 'summarize "
                   "the document' has little semantic overlap with the "
                   "document's actual content and will otherwise score low "
                   "or trigger a false fallback recommendation. Reports a "
                   "fallback_recommended flag (with a plain-language reason) "
                   "when nothing found is a confident match (or, in "
                   "fetch_all mode, when nothing has been ingested under that "
                   "session_id yet), so the caller knows to try elsewhere "
                   "and can explain why.")
    capabilities = ["knowledge-retrieve"]

    dag = DAG([
        Step("embed_query", depends_on=[], description="Embed the query text"),
        Step("search", depends_on=["embed_query"], description="Query ChromaDB for nearest chunks"),
        Step("assess", depends_on=["search"], description="Decide fallback recommendation"),
    ])

    def run(self, payload: TaskPayload) -> TaskResult:
        query = str(payload.inputs.get("query", "")).strip()
        fetch_all = bool(payload.inputs.get("fetch_all", False))

        session_id = payload.inputs.get("session_id")
        session_id = str(session_id).strip() if session_id else None
        has_where_filter = bool(session_id)

        # Optional: scopes fetch_all (see below) to ONE specific document
        # within the session, rather than every document tagged with that
        # session_id. Without this, a session with multiple uploaded files
        # (e.g. "compare the two") has no way to fetch just one of them --
        # every fetch_all call for that session returns ALL files in it
        # merged together, regardless of which file the caller intended.
        # Not used outside fetch_all mode -- similarity search already
        # naturally surfaces the most relevant chunks across a session's
        # documents without needing a hard per-document filter.
        doc_id = payload.inputs.get("doc_id")
        doc_id = str(doc_id).strip() if doc_id else None

        # fetch_all mode: for "summarize the whole document" style goals
        # against a large upload (too big to inline -- see the platform
        # controller's rule 0), similarity search is the wrong tool.
        # "summarize the document X.pdf" has almost no semantic overlap
        # with the document's actual content, so it scores low and
        # legitimately-present content gets reported as fallback_recommended
        # even though the document IS there. fetch_all bypasses ranking
        # entirely: a direct metadata-filtered fetch of every chunk tagged
        # with this session_id, no query embedding computed, no
        # min_similarity threshold applied. Requires session_id -- fetching
        # the ENTIRE shared knowledge base at once (no session scope) isn't
        # supported, and almost certainly isn't what was intended.
        if fetch_all:
            if not session_id:
                return TaskResult(task_id=payload.task_id,
                                  status=TaskStatus.FAILURE,
                                  error_msg=("fetch_all requires session_id -- fetching the entire "
                                             "shared knowledge base at once isn't supported"))
            try:
                collection = get_collection()
                # ChromaDB's flat multi-key dict form for `where` isn't
                # consistent across versions -- the explicit "$and" list
                # form is valid everywhere, so always use it once doc_id
                # narrows the filter beyond session_id alone.
                where_filter = (
                    {"$and": [{"session_id": session_id}, {"doc_id": doc_id}]}
                    if doc_id else
                    {"session_id": session_id}
                )
                raw = collection.get(where=where_filter, include=["documents", "metadatas"])
                raw = raw or {}
                ids = raw.get("ids") or []
                docs = raw.get("documents") or []
                metas = raw.get("metadatas") or []
            except Exception as e:
                # Same ChromaDB/hnswlib quirk as the semantic-search path
                # below can show up here too (a metadata filter matching
                # zero items on a small collection) -- same treatment:
                # zero results, not a hard failure.
                err_text = str(e).lower()
                if "error finding id" in err_text or "error executing plan" in err_text:
                    ids, docs, metas = [], [], []
                else:
                    return TaskResult(task_id=payload.task_id,
                                      status=TaskStatus.FAILURE,
                                      error_msg=f"ChromaDB fetch failed: {e}")

            chunks = []
            for chunk_id, text, meta in zip(ids, docs, metas):
                meta = meta or {}
                chunks.append({
                    "chunk_id": chunk_id,
                    "text": text,
                    "similarity": None,  # not applicable -- fetched directly, not ranked
                    "doc_id": meta.get("doc_id"),
                    "source": meta.get("source"),
                    "chunk_type": meta.get("chunk_type"),
                    "section_path": meta.get("section_path"),
                    "chunk_index": meta.get("chunk_index"),
                })
            # Reading order matters for a coherent whole-document summary --
            # there's no relevance ranking in this mode, so sort by original
            # position in the document instead of similarity.
            chunks.sort(key=lambda c: c["chunk_index"] if c["chunk_index"] is not None else 0)

            full_text = "\n\n".join(c["text"] for c in chunks if c["text"])
            truncated = False
            if len(full_text) > MAX_FETCH_ALL_CHARS:
                full_text = full_text[:MAX_FETCH_ALL_CHARS] + "\n\n[... truncated, document continues beyond this point ...]"
                truncated = True

            if not chunks:
                fallback_recommended = True
                fallback_reason = (
                    f"No chunks matching doc_id={doc_id!r} were found for this session's uploads."
                    if doc_id else
                    "No documents matching this session's uploads have been ingested yet."
                )
            else:
                fallback_recommended = False
                fallback_reason = None

            return TaskResult(
                task_id=payload.task_id,
                status=TaskStatus.SUCCESS,
                outputs={
                    "query": query or None,
                    "results": chunks,
                    "full_text": full_text,
                    "truncated": truncated,
                    "best_similarity": None,
                    "fallback_recommended": fallback_recommended,
                    "fallback_reason": fallback_reason,
                },
            )

        if not query:
            return TaskResult(task_id=payload.task_id,
                              status=TaskStatus.FAILURE,
                              error_msg="inputs.query is required")

        try:
            top_k = int(payload.inputs.get("top_k", DEFAULT_TOP_K))
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            top_k = DEFAULT_TOP_K
        top_k = max(1, min(top_k, 50))

        try:
            min_similarity = float(payload.inputs.get("min_similarity", DEFAULT_MIN_SIMILARITY))
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            min_similarity = DEFAULT_MIN_SIMILARITY

        try:
            query_vec = embed_query(query)
        except Exception as e:
            return TaskResult(task_id=payload.task_id,
                              status=TaskStatus.FAILURE,
                              error_msg=f"Embedding the query failed: {e}")

        try:
            collection = get_collection()
            query_kwargs = {
                "query_embeddings": [query_vec],
                "n_results": top_k,
                "include": ["documents", "metadatas", "distances"],
            }
            # Scopes the search to just this goal's uploaded doc(s) instead
            # of the whole knowledge base, when the caller supplies one.
            # Docs ingested without a session_id (i.e. everything that
            # isn't a goal-attached upload) simply have no session_id key
            # at all, so this filter naturally excludes them too -- it's
            # never accidentally matching an empty/None value.
            if has_where_filter:
                query_kwargs["where"] = {"session_id": session_id}
            raw = collection.query(**query_kwargs)
            # Some ChromaDB versions return None (rather than raising) for
            # edge cases like a near-empty/freshly-created collection --
            # treat that as "zero results" rather than crashing on the next
            # line's .get() call. Keeping this INSIDE the try block (unlike
            # the previous version of this code) means any such anomaly
            # becomes a clean FAILURE below, not an unhandled crash that
            # bypasses our error handling entirely.
            raw = raw or {}
            ids = raw.get("ids") or [[]]
            docs = raw.get("documents") or [[]]
            metas = raw.get("metadatas") or [[]]
            dists = raw.get("distances") or [[]]
            ids, docs, metas, dists = ids[0], docs[0], metas[0], dists[0]
        except Exception as e:
            # ChromaDB/hnswlib has a documented quirk: a `where` metadata
            # filter that matches zero (or very few) items on a small
            # collection can raise an internal index error ("Error
            # executing plan: ... Error finding id" or similar) instead of
            # cleanly returning empty results. Functionally this IS "no
            # matches for this filter" -- e.g. a session_id nothing has
            # been ingested under yet -- so when a where filter was active,
            # treat this class of error as zero results (falls through to
            # the normal fallback_recommended path below) rather than
            # failing the task outright, which would otherwise cascade
            # into every task downstream that depends on this one. A
            # genuine embedding/connection failure still isn't caught by
            # this narrow check and fails loudly as before.
            err_text = str(e).lower()
            looks_like_empty_filter_quirk = (
                has_where_filter
                and ("error finding id" in err_text or "error executing plan" in err_text)
            )
            if not looks_like_empty_filter_quirk:
                return TaskResult(task_id=payload.task_id,
                                  status=TaskStatus.FAILURE,
                                  error_msg=f"ChromaDB query failed: {e}")
            ids, docs, metas, dists = [], [], [], []

        # Cosine space in Chroma reports *distance* = 1 - cosine_similarity,
        # so similarity is recovered as 1 - distance.
        results = []
        for chunk_id, text, meta, dist in zip(ids, docs, metas, dists):
            meta = meta or {}  # defend against a per-chunk None metadata entry
            results.append({
                "chunk_id": chunk_id,
                "text": text,
                "similarity": round(1 - dist, 4),
                "doc_id": meta.get("doc_id"),
                "source": meta.get("source"),
                "chunk_type": meta.get("chunk_type"),
                "section_path": meta.get("section_path"),
            })

        results.sort(key=lambda r: r["similarity"], reverse=True)
        best_similarity = results[0]["similarity"] if results else None

        if not results:
            fallback_recommended = True
            fallback_reason = (
                "No documents matching this session's uploads have been ingested yet."
                if session_id else
                "No documents have been ingested into the knowledge base yet."
            )
        elif best_similarity < min_similarity:
            fallback_recommended = True
            fallback_reason = (
                f"Best match similarity ({best_similarity:.2f}) fell below the "
                f"confidence threshold ({min_similarity:.2f}) -- the internal "
                f"knowledge base likely doesn't cover this topic."
            )
        else:
            fallback_recommended = False
            fallback_reason = None

        # min_similarity was previously only used to compute the
        # fallback signal, not to actually filter what got returned -- a
        # weak, unrelated second-place chunk could ride along with a
        # strong top match and dilute downstream synthesis. The top match
        # (if fallback_recommended is False) is always >= min_similarity by
        # construction, so this can only trim lower-ranked entries, never
        # drop the one result that matters.
        results = [r for r in results if r["similarity"] >= min_similarity]

        return TaskResult(
            task_id=payload.task_id,
            status=TaskStatus.SUCCESS,
            outputs={
                "query": query,
                "results": results,
                "best_similarity": best_similarity,
                "fallback_recommended": fallback_recommended,
                "fallback_reason": fallback_reason,
            },
        )


agent = KnowledgeRetrieveAgent()

agent.generate_manifest(
    endpoint=AGENT_ENDPOINT,
    input_schema={
        "query": {"type": "string", "required": True},
        "top_k": {"type": "integer", "required": False},
        "min_similarity": {"type": "number", "required": False},
        "session_id": {"type": "string", "required": False},
        "fetch_all": {"type": "boolean", "required": False},
        "doc_id": {"type": "string", "required": False},
    },
    output_schema={
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            # Only None in fetch_all mode when no query text was supplied
            # (fetch_all bypasses the query entirely) -- see `query or None`
            # in the fetch_all branch above.
            "query": {"type": "string", "nullable": True},
            "results": {"type": "array"},
            # Only present at all in the fetch_all branch -- the normal
            # semantic-search branch has no single full_text field.
            "full_text": {"type": "string", "required": False},
            "truncated": {"type": "boolean", "required": False},
            # None whenever there are zero results to rank (both branches).
            "best_similarity": {"type": "number", "nullable": True},
            "fallback_recommended": {"type": "boolean"},
            # None on the confident-match path, in both branches.
            "fallback_reason": {"type": "string", "nullable": True},
        },
    },
)

app = build_flask_app(agent, BYOA_SECRET)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    print("=" * 60)
    print(f"Knowledge Retrieve Agent -- {agent.name} v{agent.version}")
    print("=" * 60)
    print(f"  Listening on : http://0.0.0.0:{PORT}")
    print(f"  Public via   : {AGENT_ENDPOINT} (cloudflared, managed by systemd)")
    print(f"  Chroma path  : {CHROMA_DB_PATH} (collection: {CHROMA_COLLECTION}, read-only usage)")
    print(f"  GET  {AGENT_ENDPOINT}/health")
    print(f"  GET  {AGENT_ENDPOINT}/agent.md")
    print(f"  POST {AGENT_ENDPOINT}/run")
    print("=" * 60)
    serve(app, host="0.0.0.0", port=PORT, threads=4)
